        StaticBook("An Analysis of the Socioeconomic Factors Plaguing Oren", "LeftClick", "Oren, at the moment, is in grave danger. This is not because of those most foul undead creatures, this is not thanks to the policies of the great King Sheffield, it is because of economic issues. At the moment, Oren is going bankrupt. The governing body spends upwards of 25,000 minas a day, with no real sources of income. Taxes were instituted, however briefly, and generated a negligible amount of income, but raising taxes reduces morale, so there are two obvious choices. The first is to institute taxes, and raise them to the point that they bring in 25,000 Minas day. This is obviously not preferable, and would likely cause major sources of taxable wealth to move out from Oren to the Elves or Dwarves. The second is to print money. This, in moderate amounts, would be fine for short-term economic boosts, but would, over the long term, cause deflation, which is also not preferable.  \n The third, and the option that I am proposing, is to institute a national bank. It is foolish to expect citizens to carry all of their wealth on them, and as such, a method to store money as an item, that can be reliably expected to be worth approximately what was paid for said item, is a much better system. Thus I, Atriana Tarus, although not the Financial Advisor, propose that Oren institutes a national bank, that will sell a unique item and buy it back for a slightly lower price.", ""),
        StaticBook("An Assassin's Journal", "backup_fruit", "This town is certainly a strange one. Many of the guards watch with resentful eyes, I can feel their stares. My roof climbing skills are a bit rusty, working on those is my main priority. We need but a few more Assassin's and some more money for the guild. Soon, all of Oren will know our name, the Assassini. These people truely are strange help is needed to deal with these people. I will follow them, observe their movements and actions. Maybe even practice my roof skills aswell Gathering money for the guild has been harder then expected. I may have to build it myself with what resources I have. Their castle is quite easy to sneak in to, and I currently write this from the king's throne because I was able to sneak in. They make this too easy.", ""),
        StaticBook("An Attack on Al'Khazar", "everblue2er101", "The following battle was witnessed first hand by myself, Josef Timothee, as it occured over several days in The Deep Cold, 1334. \n I awoke from a long sleep in my basement room in the VonSchlichten Inn outside of Al'Khazar and immediately felt the floor shake. I jumped up and ran outside to see lightning falling onto the capital of Oren. I ran back to my room and grabbed iron armour, an iron sword, bow, many arrows and porkchops, while placing everything else away safely.  With that done, I entered Al'Khazar. \n The city was in a state of utter chaos. Lightning continued to strike everywhere, hitting buildings, starting fires and burning those who were struck. The defenders of the city were a mix of all races. The enemies were also varied - I did not see any Undead with my own eyes, but the monsters were seemingly unlimited. Immediately, I was attacked by a swarm of small creatures, all on fire. There were also dead corpses and bones reanimated by Iblees dark power, along with giant spiders and those strange Pigmen. The tall black men were there in droves, and it was one of these that very nearly killed me. The most feared enemy were the 'Ghasts', which I saw for the first time. I battled for a long time, using my sword when it was easy to do so but attacking the Ghasts and Pigmen with arrows. \n Undead supporters taunted everyone else, claiming that Iblees would take over the city and all of Aegis. Their cries were drowned out by those fighting the Undead. Then, a miracle. Aeriel spoke, saying that she would help in the battle. With that, the tide turned, and the monsters started to recede. It seemed that the battle was won and Al'Khazar was saved... I was, at this point, standing at the Al'Khazar bank, where I had been shooting Ghasts and slaying zombies. Then I heard shouting from the Keep. \n I ran up to the great structure to a horrible sight. The guard tower on the Keep had been transformed into a hideous creation with lava pouring from it. in the Keep, the situation was even worse. Undead had barricaded themselves into the throne room and defaced the throne. From there, they mocked us, stating that Iblees would prevail. They unleashed more Ghasts and lightning outside, then vanished. \n The Keep was searched and found empty. The city was damaged and there was great loss of life, but Al'Khazar was saved. In her final words, Aeriel said that this had gone on long enough. It was time, she declared, to enter the Nether and destroy the Undead once and for all. \n My part in the battle was small. I struck down monsters with bow and blade, and joined those calling out in support of Aeriel. I swear that this account is true to the best of my knowledge.", ""),
        StaticBook("An Autobiography", "DanielDJD", "eraseall . Where should I start..... only right area to start would be the beginning I suppose. I was a peasnt, young lad, only 18 when I ventured to Alkhazar . I was poor, I had no food, and no home. For days I walked endlessly around the streets untill I found Banok_Gray I found him in Dawns Inn, a popular bar and inn. We talked polotics and war stratigies for hours. As that night ended I saw my reflection in an Ale and thought \"I need  ajob\" Met the king of Alras today, nice guy", ""),
        StaticBook("An Elf's Story", "ForhavuMahea", "Storms were gathering. The pitter-patter of rain and the distant thunder foreshadowed something to come. And something did. \n Galloping horses treaded through the muddy terrain, their riders holding swords made from the purest diamond, as well as their shining armour. \n \"Regiment!  Halt!\" Yelled the lead rider, pulling his horse to a stop, while the others followed. \n  \"Dismount!\"  Said the leader, and they dismounted their horses. \n \"This is it, men.\" \n -------- \n \n A crying child was being cradled by his mother, while the father looked frightened and watching the window suspiciously. \n \"Adda! Keep him quite! He'll give our location away!\" the father snapped at his wife. \n \"He's just a child, Herold!\"  she cried, and got up to leave the room. \n \n \"Bandits!  This is the Royal Guard!  Remove yourselves from the hut immediatly!!\" \n Inside the hut, Adda said to her husband \"Just give ourselves up.  Save Alkenaar\" \n The two left their hiding place and confronted the guards. \n \"Why did you do this?  We were your friend, Tyronis!\"  \n \"I am no friends of any filthy band like you, Addaline\" spat the leader.  \n \"We will surrender as long as you promise not to harm our son\" \n \"Oh, we wont harm your son,\" said the Guard. \"But I cant speak for the monsters of the wilderness...\" he said with a grin. \n \"As for you two,\" said Tyronis, \"I won't be taking prisoners tonight...\" \n \n --20 years later-- \n \n \"Give me the money, our you will find the blade of this sword through our stomach and back \" \n \"Here\" cried the drawf, and handed him all his money.  Alkenaar waved him off, and he ran back off to the drawf capital, thanking the Gods for sparing his life. \n Alkenaar smiled, still looking at the money, and pulled out his bow from behind his back, and shot the man down, never even glancing up.  Being an elf served him well. \n The man cried for help, struggling to the capital city, barely alive.", ""),
        StaticBook("An elf's Tale, From Laurelin to the North", "huwbert98", "I was a simple elf... But like many other young I wanted to go to war. Some ask me what do you think of the north I tell them its hell nothing like I expected \n This is my description of a number of short events This is how it begun \n I was in laurelin one day  when I found that fellow wardens wanted to go oren .We decided we would meet at the laurelin gate .We grabbed are stuff fitted are armour and set off. Are party was of Arona,Turin,Me and a mystreious figure\n \n We encountered zombies and an asortment of the undead on the way .Some of are party were wounded. Later we stopped at a great warriors pub for a rest.He as very helpful. The next day we carried on refreshed and got the kingdom of oren. I quickly warned by companions to shealth their weapons due to the fine of carrying weapons. We found that nothing of intrest had happened and explained we were scouting undead. four Humans joined are group and we set off to the north despite the calls of danger we got.", ""),
        StaticBook("An Elysian Song", "Alectriciti", "My soul was so intended. \n To save her from the undead. \n Although I was weak, \n It's her I seek. \n \n \n Then she spat in my face. \n Deep down there was grace. \n But pardon the lore. \n It's her I adore. \n \n \n Was it the sign that unslaved? \n Or the message that saved? \n Taking her hand, and running from death, \n I could feel corruption coming out of her breath. \n \n \n Determined to cleanse the vile grime, \n I did not give up. At last it was time! \n I showed her my love, she took it to clutch. \n She removed that sin - I love her so much! \n \n \n I'm seeking her always, the beat of her heart. \n 'til death intervenes, we'll never depart. \n No more ignorance, no more strife. \n Our stories extend to our afterlife. \n \n \n Such a wonderful momented happened at home, \n I can't help but write it in this tome. \n \n \n \n \n It's what we desired, yet we were surprised. \n Our sorrows unmired, our hearts harmonized: \n   \n \n \n \n        \n  \n \"Put your life where it belongs, sing elysian songs. \n Take my hand and we'll glide. Share the love you cannot hide. \n Out love's undefined \n Our hearts intertwined... \n A celestial endeavor. \n I'll hold you forever.\"", ""),
        StaticBook("An Expedition North, to the Root of Evil.", "Respiren", "A reaccount.\n \n Word had reached our ear's in the fair city of Laurelin that Winterfall had fallen \n", ""),
        StaticBook("An Explorer's Field Guide To The Nether", "l337Ninja", "Hell, or as mortals call it, The Nether, is a very dangerous place. Lava lakes that stretch for miles, fields of blood-rock burning forever, even the sand will try to drag you down into its cold, dark grip. The lost souls of the slain pigs come back to gain revenge. The spirits of mighty demons fly above to rain unholy fire upon a firey plain. If you want to survive, you must be prepared, both physically and mentally. Before you set off, you must, if you wish to survive, bring the materials neccesary to fight off the demons of Hell. You will need pure, untainted food, weapons of high quality iron or better, and a way out. Mentally you must be preparred as well. You should have your litanys ready for your journey into the realm of Iblees. For he will try to lead you to temptation, and if he can get that foothold in your mind, you are surely lost. The natural geography of Hell vastly differs from ours. The world is a giant series of caves, most larger then Kal'Ulduan in size and volume. The ground is covered in the blood of past victims and sacrifices to the Dark God. This rock is simply called \"Netherack\". The spirits of monsters long since slain in service to Him reside now in the mud, earning its nickname, \"Soul Sand\". This mud will try to bring you down into it, so that they may please their master with one more victim. The souls of the mud will appear to mess with your head, not focus on them if you wish to keep your sanity. The souls of pure quality, on the other hand, collect in bright stalactites on the roof of the caves, trying to get out. You may collect this stone to bring to the surface world. The most dangerous part of the Nether is the leaching of your soul. The long er you stay there the more your soul will bind to his realm. So you must always remember your way out, for it is your only hope of salvation. -Eliphas Faratul, Bidding you the best of luck.", ""),
        StaticBook("An History on the Followers of Aeriel", "Slayer15855", "An History on the Followers of Aeriel \n As told by Nymphix \n \n \n \n -Transcribed by the Arcane Mage- \n *Nymphix walks into a wide tavern full of the smell of Booze and the cackling laughter of ladies of the night** *Sits down in a wooden chair barely supporting his weight and creaks under the pressure all the while pulls out a small pouch and pipe and starts to fill it* \n", ""),
        StaticBook("An Invitation", "Gropier", "You are cordially invited to The Knighting Ceremony of Sir Wyvernos (Sir_Wyvernos) and Bito Minesmasher (DarkSoundwave) at The Alrasian Palace in Alras on (IRL Date/Time - Saturday, October 29th, 2011 at 8:00 PM ET -500 GMT).\n Immediately following the knighting ceremony, there will be food beverages and a performance by a local theater troupe.\n Please dress appropriately. \n Sincerely, \n \n Gordok Yon Hammer, Viceroy of Alras", ""),
        StaticBook("An ivy embroidered note", "shiftnative", "At the foot of the waterfall in Old Laurelin on the eastern side there is a tunnel to south laurelin, down the tunnel on the right hand side is a smallr tunnel to a grove. There is your next clue, Lirinya.", ""),
        StaticBook("An official greeting", "Zefmc", "It is sad to here of your uncles demise and we offer you our condolences. But time must move on I would like to meet you to descuss legal works here in the town Please leave a time convienent for you in my mail -Charon hmm", ""),
        StaticBook("An old dirty book", "Hwaldar", "After exhumating Horen's tomb,i found a book which leads to some spot. \nThe clues said i could find the \"next step\" at the Tree of Knowledge. \nThat book is lost,but i never found the tree. \nPerhaps the Elves have grown it?", ""),
        StaticBook("An Old Friend", "Jallentime", "I will miss this library. What was once a dream, a reality, and now a memory. Farewell, old friend.", ""),
        StaticBook("An old Map Piece", "MarieC90", "-You unfurl the large, dusty piece of parchment. The top section seems to depict a southern coast full of trees, which any elf would recognize as the southernmost coast of Malinor. South of the coast there are deep blue seas, dotted with craggy rocks and whirlpools. A curvy red line departs from the coast, off the left side of the map, then back again at the very bottom. The line then drops off the bottom of the page. You notice the left and bottom edges of the page seem torn.-", ""),
        StaticBook("An Old Map Piece, 2", "MarieC90", "help -You unfurl the large, dusty piece of parchment. The top section seems to depict a southern coast full of trees, which any elf would recognize as the southernmost part of Malinor. South of the coast is deep blue seas and several islands. A curvy line of red comes onto the page from the right top third of the page, curving around the islands and then dropping off the right side of the page, towards the bottom. You notice the right and bottom of the page appear torn.-", ""),
        StaticBook("An Old Map Piece, 3", "MarieC90", "-You unfurl the large, dusty piece of parchment. The top section depicts a large ocean, filled with craggy islands and the occasional red skull and crossbones. A curvy red line drops from the top of the page, winding around and avoiding the islands and skulls, then disappearing off the left side of the page. You notice the parchment is torn on the left and top sides. On the bottom of the page, near the left side, you can barely make out the following: \n find a new land. \n but beware. \n flee from \n ing compared \n cursed, so beware.", ""),
        StaticBook("An Old Map Piece, 4", "MarieC90", "-You unfurl the large, dusty piece of parchment. The top section depicts a large ocean with rocky land jutting out from the left side. The ocean is full of whirlpools and craggy rocks, and at the bottom of the page there is a large open mass. A curvy red line appears from the right side of the page, winding around the many obstacles before ending at a red X ont he land mass at the bottom of the page. You notice the top and right side of the parchment appear torn. Near the bottom of the page, on the right side, you can barely make out the following: \n For you to \n Settle there \n The danger you \n Is noth \n These lands are", ""),
