        StaticBook("Book 1: The Changing of the Nether", "itechify", "\nIblees was banished to the Nether a place of darkness and cold. Lit by cold blue stones the denize ns of the Nether were few. A large tribe of Pigmen lived in caves huddled around everlasting fires o n the ground. Evil souls who came here became floating ghosts constantly burning in pain. \nThe landscape was full of snow and large pillars of ice flowing down into large cold lakes frozen. It was a place where time was stopped eternally. \nIblees wanted revenge for the indignity of being stuck in the frozen land. He practiced his magic on the local pigmen, but was able to do nothing more than kill them. He needed a way to amplify his powers. He searched beneath a frozen lake for anything that might help him. He found a solution. \nHe found that beneath the lake lied gold. He thought maybe by using this mineral that was so pure it may be useful to his powers. He funneled his power through the minerals. What Iblees did not know was that the entire Nether was covered in gold. It encased it, like armour. \nHe funneled his powers through the gold, and the entire Nether was affect with change. The ground melted and became more like rock, setting fire in many areas. The cold blue stones that lit the Neth er turned golden with a brighter light. The frozen lakes and pillars suddenly burst into flames chan ging into a fiery liquid. The frozen souls began to move reaching up at all who would pass. The whol e of the Nether began to exude a red mist of Iblees magic that fed upon the any who entered. \nThe denizens of the Nether were changed as well by Iblees dark magic. The Pigmen that Iblees had t ested his powers on rose up again and used the new found mineral to build weapons to fight any who w ould harm them. The ghosts found they could suddenly shoot fire and would temporarily relieve their pain. \nThey all feared Iblees and what he might do to them. But Iblees spoke from the bottom of the Nethe r. \"As long as you protect me and my minions you shall never need to fear me.\" \nThey asked of him, \"Lord Iblees how shall we know who belongs to you?\" \nHe answered, \"They shall be marked with the very flames of the Nether and shall walk the Nether it self without harm.\" \nThe denizens agreed and built a shrine in his honour where his presence rules the Nether from. \nIblees became the Lord of the Nether and began to create magics so that he may gather followers. O ne day, he hoped to rule all of Aegis and strike down those who had banished him here. \nSo he began the creation of a portal....", ""),
        StaticBook("Book 2 Enor's failures", "leeloongsam", "To look into the material world we can see that the boy Enor has one crowning achievement. That is the extra district of kindling in the ruins of Al'Khazar, and being the only king to rule over one town. For this, we must thank him. His lasting contribution to humanity and Aegis will never be missed, and the world will never be the same because of it. \n \n Truth is, all the boy king has truly done is shatter the honor of our once proud nation and has left us groveling in the ashes like rats. When the council of Kramroe came pleading for aide from bandits and undead activity at Wrath's Clutch; it was ignored. A town of Oren fell to the undead, the great evil that terrorized the northern towns for years, because of the ignorance of one man in power. \n \n When war with the orcs and elves fell upon Oren the strong and mighty Enor met them with the worst resistance possible. The orcs occupied the ruins of Kramroe without a fight and harrassed human travellers before finally seizing the capital of Al'Khazar! Enor's answer to this war was to spend FIFTY THOUSAND MINAS of Oren's treasury to buy Kramroe (Now Galahar) from the orcs! And trade the Whisper Isles for it! Our own town! Bought! Horen rolled in his grave, and Oren was shattered that day. To top this off, Enor's promise to give the city of Lunavara his blessing was never acted upon, and in one fell swoop of foolishness the humans were further divided because of Lunavara's succession form Oren. \n \n For a small time things functioned within Oren, until the appointment of a council. These select few would have organized Oren to optimal functionality if they were given the chance to work. But instead, Enor fled to Winterfell, giving no direction to the council and not allowing them the ability to properly help him rule Oren. From that day in Enor vanished from the capital in favour of lonely Winterfell \n \n He does not rule for the benefit of Oren! He does not want to see it grow in glory! Only to see it wither in stagnation and unproductively while he wears a richly adorned crown. He rules in ignorance, allowing Al'Khazar, Winterfell, and New Alstion fall without much of a fight. He is the King of Weakness and Words! Oathbreaker! We need a King who will rule in STRENGTH and ACTION!", ""),
        StaticBook("Book 3: Enor and the Undead", "leeloongsam", "The blight of the undead is a powerful and enticing tool of Iblees. It is corrupted and potent to many, including the strong minded. It is the dark master's way of contorting Aegis to his will so that his followers may better control it to his liking. Brilliant men and women have fallen to this blight and turned to the Undead, and even more weak-minded men have chosen to serve them. \n \n The Sheffield's are not sparred from this taint. They are not, nor ever will be the paragons of light against the dark. This history of Undead in Oren goes hand in hand with high politics of Oren. When the Undead first appeared in the North they killed the Sympathetic King Pampo Perea, and Edmund seized the throne for himself. From there the Undead assaulted The North and sacked many northern settlements. The relative lack of action and sympathy Edmund had for the north is suspicious to say the least. Well it was until he summoned the people of Oren to court to watch him devour Queen Ivrae and join the Undead. The protector of Oren for numerous decades was simply a undead follower the entire time. \n \n A similarity occured shortly thereafter when the world of Aegis fell into a silent siege upon Enor's ascent to the throne. There was relative peace against the Undead, but it was short lived. Enor was taken from Al'Khazar with little to no fight on his part write when the city fell. He was now missing for a day or two. Not even a week. No! Enor was missing for over a year! Then suddenly he escaped with the also captured High Constable Felix. This is a farce! Few can handle a mere day in the hands of the Undead; he \"survived\" a full year of it. We are to believe our weak submissive King was not corrupted, that his sanity had not been shattered, and his view points changed. We are not a stupid people! See with your eyes and your mind! The Sheffields have done little to stop the Undead and the spread of Iblees because they worship them! Like father like son! our king has BETRAYED US!", ""),
