        StaticBook("Grog's Background Information (simonlk)", "coconuta", "Name: grog \n Job: none \n Live's in the  Wild \n Wants to work for himself \n \n Would like people to work for him, killing pigs to get him pork", ""),
        StaticBook("Growing a Grove: A Druids Guide", "Saviordude", "There might come a point in your druidc career that you will find that you are in need of your own grove. This means, of course, that you will have to grow it yourself. Many dedicants and even druids are daughnted by this task, so I have thought that it's only proper that I write a book on it. \n First off, the most common design for groves is a ring of trees surrounding a living area or shrine. The living are can be anything for a small dirt hovel to a gigantic, drudic tree, such as the one in Laurien. Another common item for a grove is a shrine or dedication. This can be a thank you to nature lovingly carved into a tree, or it can be a small stone altar. The choice is up to you. \n After those are built, a source of food is needed. I prefer a small wheat farm, just enought to feed one, maybe two people. You could also live off of making mushrooms into stew. Next, and possibly the most important part, if the flauna. Any respectable grove will have mushrooms, and sugercane too. Maybe a few vines, and you should use bone meal to speed up the growth of wild grass. And some flowers, everyone loves flowers. \n Lastly, for the more advanced druid, some standing stones can be added. Besides looking nice, they can also store druidc energy making casting certain spells easier. \n I hope that you have found this guide useful, and I wish you luck on your druidc journey!", ""),
