        StaticBook("Hail Dimitri", "LuigiNova", "It has been so long since I have heard of you I am concerened of this warning note, what foes it mean? Who is out to get you? Even me? I will arange a meeting plan for us soon, were we can discuss things. -From your pal, Tamara here", ""),
        StaticBook("Hail, I'm Raban and sadly I'm mute.", "Sidhion", "I hope you will excuse me for using this form of communication. I will answer by writing in this notebook of mine, or using some pre-written notes in another notebook most importantly: please return this notebook to me always. Thanks", ""),
        StaticBook("Halfblood Chronicles I: Birth of the Halflings", "Jerry_Man", "Stonehead, the third king of Kal'Urguan after the death of King Simmpa, was a more brazen ruler than his predecessors and took to him a human wife. Magdoline was a baron woman and fourth wife to King Stonehead, married upon the anniversary of his second year of rule.  While Stonehead bore many children he greatly loved Magdoline and desired for her to be with child. King Stonehead beseeched the help of a Druid that his wife might conceive and by Suns Smile the following year she bore Kragnook,  the first Halfling and the first known Halfblood of Aegis.\n With King Stonehead's popularity among the dwarves, many sought to marry human wives and thus began a dark time for dwarf-human relations. Men started to fear and hate the dwarfs for coming into their villages by night and stealing women away. By the time King Stonehead's reign ended and  the fourth king, Vixnor, assumed the crown the population of Halflings had grown to fourscore and six. King Dan of Al'Khazar stormed Kal'Urguan with a small force to demand the citizens of Oren be left in peace. Vixnor, who despised the marrying of dwarves and humans, agreed to outlaw the partnerships and sought to kill all living Halflings. In an act of compassion Dan rescued the Halflings and bade them to live in the outskirts of Oren. After the passing of many kings the Halflings established the township of Dunwood and the outlawing of interracial relationships was forgotten.>", ""),
        StaticBook("Halfblood Chronicles II: Walking Trees", "Jerry_Man", "The second son born of Horen, founder of men, was Impaar who towered above his brothers in size and in pride. He took to him to wed a daughter of Krug, and he loved her truly. Following the banishment of Iblees, Impaar departed from Aegis in search of other lands that he might build a city of his own, with might comparable to his father's. In the hope that he could flee Iblees' curse by leaving Aegis, Impaar never returned to his father's house. Whether he lived forever or simply died of old age, just as the men of Oren, remains unknown. But his children grew tall and taller still and the descendants of Impaar became known as Ents or 'giant', for they were tall and mighty like oak trees and their skin thick like leather.", ""),
        StaticBook("Halfblood Chronicles III: Brutes for War", "Jerry_Man", "For poor bookkeeping it is hard to tell whether Rex Lagrak'Ursin was the sixteenth or nineteenth Warlord of Krugmar, but the feat of his victory over Orgik\"Shmed, wherein his skull was smashed like a watermelon, is a popular anecdote among orc children. Lagrak'Ursin had greater plans for Krugmar and in his lack of foresight intended to declare war on every nation of Aegis. Aware that the army's might was not capable of such victory, Lagrak'Ursin set about to breed a super army of Halfbloods.\n Honourable men, elves and dwarves were belittled to conceive with brutish orc women. The resulting offspring were a variety of odd-bodies, many that were too weak to survive beyond adolescence. Just as Lagrak'Ursin was on the verge of a successful brealthrough in combining a Mountain Dwarf with a Shaman, Warlord Krimpvlark lopped the Rex's head clean off and assumed control of Krugmar. The vile experiments were abandoned and the Halfbloods banished to live their days in The Wilds. Ostracised by even those from whence they descended, the the Halfblood Orcs became known as Brutes; violent bandits and thugs who murdered travellers unprovoked, for their hatred of the pure races waxed strong.", ""),
        StaticBook("Halfblood Chronicles IV: The Daemon Prohet", "Jerry_Man", "There is a tale, the reliability of which I cannot discern, that before the War against the Undead there was a Druid whose wisdom could not stagger his curiosity. It is said that the hand of Iblees delivered a book into the Druid's posession which spoke of an ancient dark magic. From the intricately scrawled words came a horrible whispering that tantalised his mind. Altogether consumed by the shadow's grip the Druid used the book to summon Akraek: Beauty's Fury. But the Daemon refused to harness its great power to ensnare Aegis and instead seduced the Druid. \nUpon conception the man was consumed by fire and the Daemon returned to Iblees' side no longer but one entity. Within the tainted bowels of the Nather's most alluring Aemon grew Rott; destined not for his flesh father's time, but to emerge on the cusp of Iblees' resurrection.", ""),
        StaticBook("Halfblood Chronicles V: The Forgotten Elvish Cousin", "Jerry_Man", "For nigh on thirty years during the reign of High Prince Ralian, Orc diplomat Kragbar'Krump lived in Upper Laurelin and took to him an Elven wife. The practice of Elves laying with Orcs had never before been known and hopefully never would be again. So the child was named Govlen, meaning 'unlikely' and was raised in Laurelin. \nThe Boy was rich in vigor and lackluster in hair. His clumsy scaley body would break windows, knock over market stalls and mistakenly set fire to treetop houses. When it came time for his passage to become a man the Elven Council ruled that Govlen be removed from Malinor upon his return, lest the ancient forest be brought to its knees. \nGovlen lived longer than any Orc and took to him many wives of Kal'Urguan. His sprightly fertility bore 18 children, the first of whom was Kemal, who never took to him a child. His remaining sons bore many children and by the third generation the descendants of Govlen were numbered at four hundred two score and three. They dispersed themselves across Kal'Urguan becoming stall vendors and politicians, never fearful to leech extra minas from dim-witted Orcs. The Orc brutality for language referred to the Govlen family as 'Goblins', a name which has stuck with them since. As the bloodline continued Govlen's blessing of long life and fertility faded until the Goblins became almost synonymous to their Orcish cousins, save their intellectual and physical differences.", ""),
        StaticBook("Halflin Kabob", "Eltyer", "Dis be cook Gezmar, dis recipe be fer da best snack in da Krugmar... alflin kabob. Ge a alflin, and ge im to yer hut. Be carefull when ye pull 'is head off, ye nud want ta grab him at da eyes. It ruins da taste... Den, put him in oil, made wid sum herbs. Put 'im above da fire, and let it roast. In da shortest time he be done fer da eatin', share 'im wid sum brethern. And enjoy da meal! You can always eat da uncooked head afterwards, to leave a shokin taste! Dis be a great old orc recipe, and easy to make. Gud huntin' 'umie", ""),
        StaticBook("Halfling town", "henrya15", "hello king i was wondering if you could grant me land to start a halfling town near kings road. I understand there isnt a lot of space and that there is already the town of dunwood but dunwood is so far away and is not that populated. All races will be let in even orcs if the behave themselfs. since us halflings are such good farmers we could give oren 75% of all crops harvested. If you have questions or concerns please tell me Henry_Johnson (henrya15)", ""),
        StaticBook("Halfling Translation", "jessbot", "Orgulas a halfling that loves to farm and tinker with tools and armour . One day Orgulas was out in the wild hunting for some mushrooms . He found a cave and went to look for some mushrooms in the cave. After about an housr of minning he grew to detest the rock and grimy coal he found in the cave. He got angry and slamed at some rock he was standing on with his pick. The rock started to rumble and it gave way plunging orgulas into an underwater stream. He was washed deep into the caves, until he grabed a thing that was hanging of the edge of the canal . As he did this the dwarf skeleton he grabed onto fell onto him which natually startled the poor Half fling. \n Afterhitting his head on the rock and passing out Orgulas woke to a dark cavern. He felt something on him and lit a torch, Again the dwarf skeleton startled Orgulas making him drop his torch which rolled off to the edge of a ravine. Having no equipment Orgulas took what he could from the dwarf leaving his clothes behind. 2 years he spent in that cave system eating fungi and rotten flesh. He began to forget what he knew about the surface, Farming, Fishing, and Woodcutting was pointless. So soon he forgot every thing about them. But he started to learn things about the rock and ores. \n This was the story of a Halfling how turned away from farming to become closer to his dwarf relatives.", ""),
        StaticBook("Hallbjorn's Journal, Vol. I", "Snowy_Fox", "I have never been one for writing. So the Lord only knows what possessed me to make a journal. I suppose it is merely to pass the nights away... life in the wilds necessitates huddling in a shelter through the dark hours, unless you would rather see yourself at the end of a skeleton's blade. I prefer to tackle them when the sun rises, and the sun's rays aid their defeat. Speaking of which, it is getting late. I had better rest, lest I arise too late on the morrow to meet my quarry. \n \n \n \n For some time, I have had a semi-permanent home established, far to the south of the towns. My hope is that it is far enough afield that the bandits won't come across it, but even so I will not grow complacent, and I never feel quite safe there. I avoid keeping valuables in there as well, unless I am around to keep eyes on the place. Other times I will move anything I would sorely miss into one of my hidden caches. \n Now is such a time. I am on one of my expeditions, exploring the shore eastwards from my home this time. I set sail by boat, and had hoped that my wolf, Yrsa, would follow along the shore, but she did not. I hope she returned home and is safe. \n \n \n \n", ""),
        StaticBook("Halls of the Blind", "dagothagahnim", "I can see what you see not... \nVision milky, then eyes rot... \nWhen you turn, they will be gone... \nWhispering their hidden song... \nThen you see what cannot be... \nShadows move, where light should be... \nOut of Darkness, Out of Mind... \nCast down, into the Halls of the Blind...", ""),
        StaticBook("Hallucination", "tyking919", "", ""),
