        StaticBook("Wanted Report", "mylimo7", "Botar: Harvesting Undead, Aiding Undead, Resisting Arrest, Plotting with Undead. \n \n Reward: (To be confirmed by King Enor) \n \n \n \n Will_Forestin: Aiding Undead, Plotting with Undead. \n \n Reward: (To be confirmed by King Enor) Vardak, Lucas Black. Thral'Mar.", ""),
        StaticBook("War", "redbaron", "The tired soldier rode his horse through the town gates. It was a small town, but one in the enemy's path. A hundred thousand orcs were headed towards them. He rode swiftly from town to town, warning the people, but they refused to listen. They were stubborn folk and refused to believe that their peace was ending. Orcs had not been seen in this land for over a hundred years, why would they come now? Hope was fleeing from the soldier's eyes. These were his people and they were too stubborn to listen to one of their own.Orcs had not been seen in this land for over a hundred years, why would they come now? Hope was fleeing from the soldier's eyes. These were his people and they were too stubborn to listen to one of their own. He tugged his hood back over his head, coming in doggedly from the edge of town to the center to make his announcement. At the town's center well, he stopped his horse and dropped out of his saddle. His soft brown boots were stained and travel worn, but made no noise nor print on the ground. His long sword thumped against his side and loose chain mail shirt inside it's plain scabbard. His cloak masked him well and fell evenly to the His soft brown boots were stained and travel worn, but made no noise nor print on the ground. His long sword thumped against his side and loose chain mail shirt inside it's plain scabbard. His cloak masked him well and fell evenly to the ground as he stepped down. He was tall, as high as his horse, but needed more height to gain the eyes of the stubborn folk he now had to speak with. He kept his hood on as he cleared his throat to speak.ground as he stepped down. He was tall, as high as his horse, but needed more height to gain the eyes of the stubborn folk he now had to speak with. He kept his hood on as he cleared his throat to speak. It had been quite a while since he used it. \"People of Ceritan!\" He spoke loudly and with authority to gain their attention and thought. \"I bring news from outside your lands!\" People started to gather around him, eager for news, much more for curiosity sake than interest. He cleared his voice throat again to keep it going. \"Orcs are coming from the northern passage! A hundred thousand strong! Heed me and come out of the valley to the high lands for safety!\" Immediately people laughed. One came out and started arguing with him. \"Orcs?! In this valley?! You're been drinking too much ail good man!\" \"There has been no sign of orc nor troll since our founder's days!\" a man in back shouted. \"There is no fear of such creatures here! Go back to your king jester!\" The soldier stood firm. \"I tell you this not in jest,\" he spoke clearly, \"but in warning! If you do not come to the highlands, than all shall fail you! They are too strong for the Soldiers, Rangers or Knights! You have no hope for survival if you do not flee now!\" Many more laughed. A figure approached him, serious and not laughing, making their way cautiously through the crowd. This caught his eye because the movements were graceful, yet very sure of themselves, a combination not known to human kind but to elves alone. The person stopped before him, clearly in view now, and startled him because of what it was. It was a human, plain as could be, with dirty ragged clothes and soot covering the face and arms. It stood before him and stared intently at him, matching his eyes perfectly, wanting to make him look away if he could. It was a challenge or a test, he could not tell, but he had to know what this creature was, for it confused him a great deal. It seemed to be a girl of sorts, but she dressed and walked like a man and stared at him squarely as an equal, no fear of man in her eyes. Her raven hair was cut much like his, barely touching her slender shoulders, but it seemed to also frame her face and brought more attention to her eyes and mouth. Her eyes were a rainbow of colors, shaped like a cats and just as unnerving, sly and clever. Her mouth perfectly shaped with full lips that knew smiles and laughter just as much as sorrow and grief. She seemed neither happy nor sad, merely watchful. Her skin was firm and golden with speckles everywhere and had no lack of scars. She seemed to be more of a man than a woman by how se stood and dressed, in plain foreign leggings and tunic, but her figure and face stated that she was indeed a girl. She stood no higher than his saddle and could be no more than twelve years old. She did not fear him nor anyone else that looked at her then. The crowd had quieted with her approach. The soldier matched her gaze, very intent and serious. At last she spoke. \"How far are they?\" Her voice was firm and fair as well and spoke of unknown authority, but it was obvious that she listened well to people by what she said. The crowd roared in laughter. \"You believe such wild tales urchin?!\" a man next to her. He laughed and almost hit her. She ducked easily away, ignoring him. He continued to banter. \"She believes him! The proud queen of urchins believes a madman who dreams of orcs!\" Laughter continued on. She spoke again. \"How far are they?\" The soldier spoke to her alone, for only she listened. \"I cannot say. I lost track after being sent to warn the towns. They were a day or so behind me, but I fear that ground is taken in.\" She nodded, thought running through her eyes. She bit one of her knuckles and looked down. \"Not much time then.\" The soldier noted her accent then. It was strange to him, and that told him much. He traveled the lands far and wide for several years and never did he hear such an accent before. She nodded again, deciding something. She looked back at him. \"Can you lead us to a safe place?\" He stared at her then. Us? He nodded, thinking she only had delusions. She was a street urchin after all. \"Yes, if you'll let me.\" She nodded again. \"Two hours,\" she said evenly. \"We'll meet you by south gate.\" She turned around quickly and darted past the people laughing. She then made sounds no one recognized and ran onward. The crowd laughed and dispersed. The soldier sighed. He had to wait those two hours now. He needed to rest himself and his horse anyway. Whether the girl showed or not, it didn't matter. One urchin, no matter how foreign, was not worth his life as of yet. The two hours passed swiftly and the soldier tugged on his hood as he made his way past the vendors to the south gate. He looked up briefly to see if the girl was there or not and gaped at seeing several more than just the girl waiting for him. We' and us' were well used. There stood before him every urchin, runaway and orphan in the whole town, totaling fifty men, women and children(mostly children). The girl he met stood at the front of him and glared a bit. \"You're late,\" she said tartly. He nodded in amazement. \"And good that I am too if so much value their lives.\" He looked around and saw cats and dogs also gathered there. Confusion spread across his face more. He looked back to the girl and she shrugged. \"Some creatures are smarter than others.\" She turned and started walking out the gate. She turned back to him. \"Well?!\" A huff and then a smile crawled across his face. He led his horse passed the others and started leading the people out of the doomed city. Hours passed under the sun. The soldier walked in silence and the people behind him followed. They walked across the wide fields that brought out great harvests every year and pass the flat lands that fed the herds. The urchins would not miss it. None of it belonged to them. Only their odd leader, the girl, looked around at them in awe. She said something in a language the soldier never heard before, one that made her voice almost angelic. He looked back at her and saw wonder in her eyes. She truly was a child. He shook his head and continued onward. \"What?\" he heard her say. \"I said nothing,\" he responded. \"You thought something,\" she responded quickly. \"It must be interesting.\" \"What would make that idea enter your mind lass?\" He looked behind him to see her, but she already caught up and was walking next to him. \"You shook your head. That means something.\" A smirk played on her face and he could tell she wanted to know more than why he shook his head. He shook it again. \"I wonder why is it you trust I so easily and look with wonder on this land?\" Her eyebrows raised. \"Why do I trust you?\" she asked him in clarification. She shrugged. \"You're honest man. Not many can look me in eye for long except best liars and most honest. I have. affect on people.\" She shrugged again. \"It scares some, intimidates others and makes rest look away in shame. You took my stare easily enough, so you must be telling truth. Liar wouldn't lead fifty-four people to death after my stare.\" She looked around. \"And land is\" she failed to find the right word and started saying word in her own language to substitute it. He understood none of it, so he guessed, throwing out words. \"Flat, empty, green, plain, pretty\" She snapped her fingers and pointed at him. \"Yes! Pretty! It's very pretty.\" \"You are a foreigner, true?\" He wished to know where and why he did not recognize her language. She nodded. \"Yes, I'm foreigner. Obvious isn't it.\" She cringed. \"I can't quite get your\" she searched for another word, flailing her hands around, trying to pantomime it. The word though was impossible to describe in hand movements. She started to curse in her language and several others he did not recognize. She eventually just made a small gap between her thumb and pointer finger, trying to make it easier. \"In between words,\" she tried. Confusion ran across the man's face until he understood. \"Oh, article words. A, an, and the.\" She snapped her finders again. \"Exactly! Those are always difficult for me. Never can quite get them right.\" She shook her head. \"It's always hard learning new language, especially without teacher or guide.\" She folded her arms and grumbled in her own language again. \"From where do you come?\" The soldier was not accustomed to talking but this girl was of interest to him now. \"I do not recognize your language and I have traveled much.\" She looked at him side long and shrugged. \"You wouldn't know place even if you traveled whole world. Too obscure. I was taken away from my home and trying to go back. Much more difficult than any could expect.\" She stretched and looked behind her. A child was catching up to them eagerly. \"Neh-chan!\" the kid cried and came up to her as she watched. The soldier looked at the girl. \"Neh-chan?\" He never heard anything like it. The girl nodded. \"I told children to call me that. It's title, not my name.\" She knelt down to the child and started to talk to it as an older sister or aunt would. The child flung its arms around her neck and the girl picked her up, a laugh in her eyes. She fell behind him, carrying the kid and marched again in relative silence. Night was drawing nearer and the company grew very tired. The soldier was more tired than any of them combined but he did not complain. The tiredness was familiar to him, and to the company it seemed. The smallest children were being carried and others were poking the others to keep themselves awake. They needed a rest though and a good camping spot. He called Neh-chan over to him and she seemed to pout. When she came to him she told him why. \"Neh-chan's for little kids to call me. You're older. Call me Hikari.\" \"Is this Hikari' your name either?\" He did not want an argument, but she seemed to. \"No. It's also title. You think I'm\" she tried to think of the right word to say what she wanted to but couldn't. She shook her head, giving up. \"It's not wise to give out my name without knowing who it is I give it too. I don't like being followed.\" \"Neither do I,\" he stated flatly. \"Hikari then. We need to make camp and this is as good a spot as any. Make sure they gather and bring wood and water. We need a fire, but only for cooking, nothing else. Keep it small.\" He left his horse with her and went off a little on his own. Hikari called out. \"What should I call you if we're in danger?\" A plausible question, he warranted, but rather late. He looked back to her. \"Cloak.\" He turned to leave them and scout the area. He heard her grumble in her own language and imagined her asking what kind of name that was. They were a league or so away now from their staring point and was amazed that they made it so far on their own. With all the children and the like, he suspected that they would be much closer to their doom, but they were much farther than he hoped He saw out onto the plains and back to the town they left. It was nothing but a blur among the fields they passed through, but a wave of black was headed quickly towards it. Fires came with the orcs and there was nothing he could do about it. He gripped his sword and grieved in his heart that the other villagers did not heed his warning and that none of the other villages he went to did either. Oh these foolish people! Were the wise the ones who starved or froze to death on the streets? What manner of men now ruled these fair lands? His questions lay unanswered and he followed their trail. It was hard to see but his vision was keen, the keenness of men. It was visible that many traveled together to the rocky slope of the mountains, enough people to feed a small company or orcs. If they had not their fill at the village, the orcs would surely come after them. And they traveled better at night, Cloak remembered. He shook his head. A single rider would be unnoticed by them, but fifty or more would gather attention. They had to move again as soon as they were rested to a more defendable position. I must gather the men, he thought, but what hope were they? None would have held a sword before and most were not married nor brothers or fathers to the other people there. He was their only hope, and he failed them. Cloak came back to the camp to find all his instructions carefully taken out. Many of the small children were asleep and others were resting their muscles as they waited for his return. Hikari was stretching her legs and teaching a few children to do the same. \"This helps them continue moving,\" she stated in plain speech. Her accent remained very clear. \"Don't bounce and don't go too far or you'll hurt them worse!\" She spotted Cloak and nodded. \"Alright, get to fire. Keep it hot. Don't sit down right away. Makes you stiff.\" The kids ran off and helped a few there with the cooking. Hikari went strait towards him. \"How bad is it?\" Cloak was taken back by this question and confusion spread across his face. \"You look worried,\" she mumbled. \"How bad is it? When do we have to leave?\" He tried to look at her, but couldn't. He turned his focus and started tending his horse. It may be the last time he ever rode her. Cloak finally spoke. \"The orcs are about to attack the village. It will not take them long to finish it off. If luck be with us, they will stay there til the moon is high. They will be full and ignore a meager fifty. If not,\" he looked down. \"If not, we have until daylight until out deaths.\" She mumbled to herself in her own language and something in her tone said she was being sarcastic to herself. \"So we count on luck,\" she tested the word, having never used it. \"And hope. You don't seem hopeful.\" He looked down. \"I have seen many villages fall. It is wearisome for me. What hope is there of defeating this evil if none believe what is said enough to help in the fight?\" He sighed and groomed his horse. \"Rarely have the orcs let a company as large as this go by unnoticed. They will come.\" The girl grinned. \"Let them come.\" He looked at her in anger and fear, expecting magic to be undone and for her to turn into an orc as well, but none of that happened. She had a grin on her face that made him know she had a plan for them. \"What is it you are planning?\" She only grinned. The moon rose and then fell. The urchins took shifts of who was awake and who was not. They worked well in the mountains and prepared themselves for the up coming battle. The plan was simple. Make them too troublesome an annoyance to deal with and make them leave them alone. They would hold out, they hoped, long enough for the opposition to be tired of them and leave. Cloak knew this would not work. Orcs were relentless once they smelled meat. The sun rose next and the sea of insufferable creatures came to them. They were the most hideous of creatures that stood on two legs; no person there could find the words to describe them than as ugly nightmares from the great abyss. Hikari though could think of plenty of words to describe them, just couldn't translate it into anything the rest understood. The orcs came, gnashing their teeth and wailing loudly. They wielded their scimitars and swords and cutlasses as foolish children with great strength. They even killed some of their own as they came up to the gathering on the mountain. The only true description of an orc was for their love of killing. The heavy black army came up the mountain slopes and was met with boulders and rocks, crushing them and making them run into each other. Hundreds died easily from that alone. They backed off a moment, then charged again. More boulders fell down hill. Several hundred more died. The enemy gnashed their teeth and howled more, upset that their next meal was fighting back so much. Some were smarter than the others and found a way up the slope and to their prey. The defense the urchins made that night had two openings: one was for escape and the other for taunt. The more orc they killed, the less there were for the soldiers waiting for them would have to face. Taunting was required for this maneuver to work well enough. It did though make them vulnerable. The taunting spot Cloak stood at and slayed each orc that tried to enter in and kill them. The confined space helped him win each time one made it there. The orc's black blood ran freely down his blade and down the hill. The second entrance though was still very vulnerable. One company of orcs made it up the hill and to the other side. Two children screamed and ran away from them, hiding as soon as they could. They were being invaded at last. The fear Cloak suppressed as the people worked that night came back and choked him as he heard the cries. The urchins did not leave their posts to investigate; they continued to throw boulders and rocks down the mount and slay orc that way. The army or a hundred thousand was diminished by half already by a mere fifty dirty humans, most of them children. Hikari was no where in sight. The orcs stepped back, away from the mountain, then started to run far from them. The invasion they started was somehow forted, though they knew not why. Cloak slayed the last orc that dared to come to the taunting hole, then left his post to where he heard the earlier cries. There he saw endless bodies of slain orcs, slain by their own weapons, but in a fashion he never saw before. The cuts were fast and sharp, hitting only vital points and killing their foes instantly. Mercy killings they were called among assassins. There was not a single human found on the ground. There was a yelp from the front of the barrier. Cloak rushed over to that area, clenching his sword earnestly. What was going on?! \"What has happened?!\" He asked immediately as he met one of the few adult men there. The man pointed to the other side of the barrier. Cloak slipped through their taunting hole and gazed on the Hikari, covered in orc blood and carrying their weapons. He stared at her, unable to understand how or when she became like this. She dropped the weapons and mumbled something in her language to herself. She didn't go towards the barrier, but towards the stream they found the other day. Cloak followed her, still amazed . Hikari leaned over the water and started to clean herself off. She kept talking to herself in her language, then switched over to common speech. \"Are you sure it's wise to leave them unguarded? Kids get restless.\" She put her head under the water and scrubbed her head clean. After a moment, she stripped off the outer layer of clothing she had on and started to clean her clothes as well. \"I knew I needed more clothes\" she grumbled to herself. She made a face, at the clothes, then looked at Cloak, still in shock. \"What's matter?\" \"Did you kill all those orcs?\" He pointed behind himself, awed by her attitude and actions. She nodded and continued to clean her clothes. \"How? You had no weapons?\" \"Where I come from,\" she explained, \"we have method of fighting with our hands and feet. I can break your arm easily if I twist it just right. Kill you even. I learned it while ago for\" she hunted for the word. \"Study,\" she said at last. \"Along with how to use swords and whips. I'm rather good at staff too, but they didn't have any. Disarming opponent easy too, if you dodge well enough.\" She put her feet in the stream. She smiled and commented in her own language. \"Better\" she mumbled as well. He stared at her, not knowing what she was anymore. \"You scared them all away by your fighting and your plan,\" he said at last. \"No one in a hundred years has managed that.\" She grinned. \"Nice I love breaking records.\" She sat back and looked at the afternoon sky. Many hours of fighting had occurred that day and she felt like a rest. \"How many dead?\" she asked evenly as she let the water massage her feet. \"It is hard to tell.\" Cloak sat down on a rock near her and cleaned his sword. \"We destroyed half the orc army at least, a miracle. And we survived it, another miracle.\" He started to sharpen his sword now. \"Now we must continue to survive. Best lead the urchins well Hikari. You are a stout and strange girl, but they trust you. Lead them beyond the mountain and make a new city there.\" She blinked and turned to him. \"Strange? Is that same as unordinary?\" Cloak nodded. She smiled. \"Good. I like being strange. But I'm not girl,\" she said at last and stood up. He gazed at her and wondered why she said such a ridiculous thing. She was obviously a girl now especially without the extra clothing she wore to protect herself during battle. She explained easily. \"Your term for girl' refers to young female. I'm young, but not that young. I'm 26. Yes I look 13, but that's\" she looked up for the word again and figured that there wasn't a translation for it at all. She cursed to herself. \"All lacking in speech,\" she mumbled, then started picking up her wet clothes, inspecting them. Cloak stared at her again. She couldn't be as old as she proclaimed. It was impossible! He shook his head then sheathed his sword. \"Whatever your age, you have earned my respect Hikari Neh-chan.\" He bowed respectfully to her, pulling his hood further over his face. No one had seen it the whole battle long. Hikari wanted to change that. As he turned back to the camp, she leapt up and pulled his hood off his head. He whirled around, his hand at his sword. She could finally get a good look at him. He was young in appearance but his eyes were old and gray. He had a trace of a beard and mustache and his hair was the same color as the bark on the trees; some of it fell across his face. Scars were scattered across it too, proving he had been to battle more than he seemed to earlier. His jaw was firm and his presence, without the hood, was authoritative and bold. He tried to stare her down, but her own odd appearance was enough to keep him at bay. She looked disappointed. \"I thought with all fuss you made about keeping hidden you'd be either pretty or ugly. You're rather plain to me.\" She threw his hood back up. \"I'm not their leader. I was passing through when you made your announcement and they treated me well while I was there. They don't have leader, they lead themselves.\" Cloak stared at Hikari, wondering and fearing what she wanted. She continued. \"Let me come with you. I need to learn and you need someone to cover your back. I learn fast and I'm light. They don't need me. They'll be fine.\" He looked at her gravely. She did not know what she asked. Before he could tell her no, she spoke again. \"I'll follow you around either way. Being called neh-chan all times is pain.\" Cloak blinked and eventually nodded. He couldn't stop her anyway. She grinned and ran ahead of him barefoot. They went back to the camp and sent the urchins of the former Ceritan to the other side of the mountain to make their new homes. Within the day, the two new companions left the others and went on to their journey. Once away from the crowd, Hikari stopped Cloak to talk for a moment. \"Since we're gonna be traveling together, let's give each other more favorable names to go by.\" Cloak looked at her. She must have been sick of being called Hikari Neh-chan a lot. He nodded in agreement and waited for hers first. \"Call me.Endi.\" The word was foreign to him, but so was all the words she went by he learned over time. \"So it be, Endi. I shall be called Esrald. Tis a name I go by.\" She tested the word then nodded. It'd do. At least until their journey together ended. ill keep that in mind", ""),
        StaticBook("WAR", "imbtrtenu", "War, the orcs have been war with the Human for as long as remembered While the orc have been busy Being Constantly Ambused but because of this the orcs Have set off to create the small town of Kel'Og. To forever settle and make shelther Until the day comes where they will be attacked!", ""),
        StaticBook("War - Volume I", "Crippledbaboon", "War, is not something one should aspire to create, but to eradicate completely. However you must not confuse yourself as others before you may have done. War must NOT be solved with fighting. It must be solved through patient, diplomatic talks of other, more humane solutions to the problems. War can be caused by many things. Relgious Belief, Money and Power. And in tales of old, it is caused by the one thing that, to this very day, Kamees, 12th of Snows Maiden, 1304, may cause a man to end his own life or start new. Love. Is the one, true cause of war. Love for land, for money, belief. One must not be blinded by love for the sake of his brothers. When you are a diplomat in a situation that could turn disastorous, you must empty your mind of all that ales you. You must become an overseer of the land. Think of what is occuring in Aegis and beyond. Think of the consequences war could bring. Then, and only then may you decide what is right for the land and your people.", ""),
        StaticBook("War - Volume II", "Crippledbaboon", "This next installment shall be on the tricks of conquest. How to build yourself a sturdy cannon. How to find your way inside a fortification and slay the damned leader of your enemies. And of course. How to raise a capable army of loyal troops. When war is afoot and you are a responsible general or at the least, high in command. You must first send out a message across the land calling for able troops to join your honourable masters army. Once all volounteers have gathered in your city square * make sure it is in your capital. It is a place of safety and history, a fine place to muster troops. * you must give an almighty speech, to rally and encourage these loyal men. War is a tough time and it calls for sacrifice. Let them know this, if they arrive with the fear of death in their minds, cast them out. For this is not were they belong. In war, each man woman and child is a target, you shall need these men to know that. They must know that in war, men give their lives to protect the people. Once your grand speech is over, ask your new soldiers to enter the local barracks for this is where they shall be assigned their new position in the army. Once all men have now been armed, armoured, fed and taught the ways of war. You must assign them around your capital positions in the streets and on the walls. Keep two or three men with your royal family at all times. Once this is done you may begin your war plan as you see fit. First you must call upon a scout to know your enemies movements and what not. To prevent your foes from doing the same send out patrols of three to 4 soldiers around the outskirts of the capital and other key locations. You must not let scouts inside or enemy soldiers. However if a peasant from enemy settlements enters your capital have them watched very carefully, ask them their business. Do not have them arrested on sight, they genuinely may be seeking a safer haven from the war. Although, this could be a spy or saboteur. Once your scout has come back from his mission, gather your smartest and most tactical men, in both war and diplomacy. And string together a grand mission. Take note, wars are not one by grand battles alone. Patience is the key, engage in small raids on settlements, clearing out the enemy and creating a sense of security in the area. Do this repeatedly to all small villages. Once you reach a town of good proportions, have a scout enter the town again. Checking the strength of the walls, the mind-state of the troops and citizens. Once this is done, have the scout return and give his review. If the walls are of ample strength, muster together able technicians to create a cannon. Now, a cannon is a very technical thing that few know how to create. It is to be made on the site of the walls, no more than 20 paces away. Any farther and it shall have no effect. The cannon must be built at the time of the siege, any earlier and the townsmen will have time to remove it. Once the cannon is built, fire into the enemy walls and allow your men to enter cautiously. Once inside the town send men out in pairs to scour the streets. Stick to alleyways and make sure to sneak, do not run. You may fall into an ambush. Once the streets are safe place one or 2 men at the broken down wall so nobody may enter behind you. With the rest of your men, gather at the main courtyard and begin searching for the towns highest of order. Eliminate them, and the town is yours. Assure the townsfolk that you mean them no harm and are fighting for the greater good of Aegis. Continue all these steps and youa re sure to win the war. Get out there soldier! Aegis needs YOU! coughs* And ale...", ""),
